#  Healthcare 30-Day Readmission Prediction

##  Project Overview

This project develops an end-to-end Machine Learning system to predict whether a patient is likely to be readmitted to the hospital within 30 days.

The project covers data preprocessing, exploratory data analysis, feature engineering, machine learning model development, hyperparameter tuning, evaluation, and prediction.

---

##  Objective

The main objective is to identify patients at higher risk of 30-day hospital readmission using historical healthcare data.

This type of predictive modeling can help healthcare organizations identify high-risk cases and support data-driven planning.

---

##  Technologies Used

- Python
- Pandas
- NumPy
- Matplotlib
- Scikit-learn
- Joblib
- Google Colab
- GitHub

---

## 🔎 Exploratory Data Analysis

The following factors were analyzed:

- Age
- Gender
- Time spent in hospital
- Previous inpatient visits
- Number of medications
- Laboratory procedures
- Number of diagnoses
- Numerical feature correlations
- Outliers
- 30-day readmission distribution

---

## 🤖 Machine Learning Models

### 1. Logistic Regression

Used as a baseline classification model.

### 2. Random Forest Classifier

Used to capture nonlinear relationships and estimate feature importance.

### 3. Hyperparameter Tuning

RandomizedSearchCV with 3-fold cross-validation was used to optimize the Random Forest model.

---

## 📊 Final Model Performance

The tuned Random Forest model achieved:

| Metric | Score |
|---|---:|
| Accuracy | 64.34% |
| Precision | 17.76% |
| Recall | 57.17% |
| F1 Score | 27.10% |
| ROC-AUC | 65.24% |

A prediction-threshold analysis was also performed to investigate whether classification performance could be improved for the minority class.

---

##  Feature Importance

Random Forest feature importance was used to identify the most influential features for the prediction task.

---

##  Prediction System

A reusable prediction function was developed that:

1. Accepts patient information
2. Applies the required preprocessing
3. Generates a readmission probability
4. Applies the selected classification threshold
5. Returns the predicted readmission risk

